life: 1.0
name: site
summary: "The known.life genepool itself — an Astro SSR app on a Cloudflare Worker that IS the registry (publish · resolve · auth · scan · gene viewer) plus the docs site. This is the one production surface every other .life depends on: install.sh is served from here, /api/resolve/<gene>/latest is how genes are inherited, and the engine ships as the `life` package's files[\"life\"]. Deploys under the convention as a ci-auto unit (it's production, default-on); the live Cloudflare resources predate the convention, so config: pins their names and binding vars (env.KNOWN_KV / env.KNOWN_R2) rather than refactoring the worker. The deploy is THIS repo's own — the canonical dogfood worst case the ci-deploy-convention was designed against."
imports:
  known.life/registry:
  # The multi-life viewer (served at /app) — same ownership cut as the
  # registry: the gene is the sole source, materialized into .genome/viewer/
  # and mounted from src/middleware.ts; this repo holds no copy.
  known.life/viewer:

  # CF-runtime config with no portable meaning — the exact pinned compatibility
  # the live worker runs on. nodejs_compat is required by the Astro CF adapter.
  known.life/cloudflare:
    compatibility_date: "2026-05-01"
    compatibility_flags:
      - nodejs_compat

  known.life/github:
    # The build above embeds the repo-root installer, so an install.sh edit must
    # trigger this unit's ci-auto deploy (engine ≥3.46.0 deploy_inputs) — without
    # it, an installer change "deploys" as a silent no-op until the next site/ touch.
    deploy_inputs:
      - install.sh
      # The lockfile: this unit's worker IS mostly vendored genes (registry, viewer
      # — materialized from these pins at CI), so a lock-only re-pin is a real code
      # change here. Without this, a gene patch published + re-pinned ships as a
      # silent no-op deploy (lived 2026-07-18: registry@0.8.1's /jwks fix sat
      # unshipped behind a green "deploy: skipped").
      - .life.lock

# The worker's security-spine unit suite — scan (the leak gate), lifekey-verify
# (the trust-store/handshake root), jwt (the write-endpoint bearer), db-accounts (the upsert behind
# /api/auth/prove). Pure WebCrypto/jose/node:sqlite, the one fetch stubbed, no
# network — a genuinely self-contained ARTIFACT test (the one the unified-CI
# `life test --changed` rail runs for site/, replacing the hand-kept worker-unit
# job). cwd is this dir, so no `cd site`.
tests:
  - "npm ci && npm run test:coverage"

requires:
  infra.compute:
    # Deploy to the live Worker script name, not the unit's (`site`). Per-resource
    # names + binding vars are pinned to the resources that predate the convention
    # (the worker reads env.KNOWN_KV / env.KNOWN_R2 / env.DB), so `life deploy`
    # binds the existing stores instead of provisioning fresh empty ones.
    # The life-dataplane service binding: this repo's own data plane (dataplane/
    # cell), reached in-process by the viewer's plane seam — same-account public
    # worker->worker fetch is CF-blocked, the binding is the contract.
    services:
      DATAPLANE: life-dataplane
    vars:
      # Which registry-gene version this deployment vendored — served as the MCP
      # handshake's `serverInfo.version`. The worker cannot know it itself (no
      # filesystem, and a gene's version is assigned at publish, not written into
      # the gene), so the cloudflare adapter resolves `{{gene:}}` from THIS repo's
      # `.life.lock` at deploy: the same pin the engine re-verifies on install, so
      # the reported version can never disagree with the code that shipped.
      # It was a literal in the gene bumped by hand — it drifted two majors once —
      # and then briefly a vite `define` here, which was the same stamping done by
      # hand when the substrate already had `vars:` (Law 1.1).
      REGISTRY_VERSION: "{{gene:registry}}"
      # The origin the viewer will present for THIS life's plane — the key the
      # transport map below matches on (src/middleware.ts). It was a hardcoded
      # `life-dataplane.i-808.workers.dev` in the worker source, a second statement of
      # a host the root `.life` already states, and a silent one: if the two ever
      # disagree the key stops matching and the viewer falls back to a public
      # worker->worker fetch that Cloudflare blocks. `{{workers_dev}}` derives the
      # account half at deploy, so the only thing written by hand is the script name —
      # which is the same `life-dataplane` the service binding above already names.
      DATAPLANE_ORIGIN: "https://life-dataplane.{{workers_dev}}"
    deploy_name: known-life
    # Build: install deps, fold the root installer into the served assets (it's
    # served at known.life/install.sh from the gene pool, not raw GitHub), then run
    # the Astro Cloudflare build → dist/_worker.js (SSR module graph) + dist/ assets.
    build: npm ci --no-audit --no-fund && cp ../install.sh public/install.sh && npm run build
    artifact: dist/_worker.js
    assets:
      dir: ./dist
      binding: ASSETS
      not_found: 404-page
    # Post-deploy smoke (engine ≥3.38.0): the genepool's two load-bearing surfaces
    # answer after every CI deploy — the installer every cold-start curls, and the
    # resolve endpoint every .life inherits through. A mismatch fails the deploy.
    verify:
      - /install.sh=200
      - /api/resolve/life/latest=200
      # The multi-life viewer's sign-in page (src/viewer, mounted at /app).
      - /app=200
    # Eyes on the one production surface every .life depends on (Law 13). `observe:`
    # is the PORTABLE observability knob (cloudflare gene ≥1.36.8) — the adapter maps
    # it to CF Workers Logs + Traces metadata. Read it back with `node scripts/
    # observe.mjs`. Logs + traces share the Workers Logs free tier (200k events/day);
    # `true` = full sampling (registry traffic is low, so well within it; use
    # `observe: { sampling: N }` to dial down if volume ever climbs).
    observe: true
  infra.route:
    # A public site on the apex custom domain. It brings its own worker, so it
    # authenticates its own privileged paths (owner-gate on publish/lifecycle, the
    # /exchange boot flow) — there is no injected gate; `public: true` is the honest
    # marker that the surface itself is world-reachable.
    route: known.life
    public: true
  infra.datastore:
    db_name: known-life-db
  infra.kv:
    kv_title: known-life-known_kv
    kv_binding: KNOWN_KV
  infra.object_store:
    bucket: known-life-known-r2
    r2_binding: KNOWN_R2
  infra.secret_store:
  infra.ai:

---

# site — the known.life genepool

This directory is **the genepool itself**: the deployable that serves
[known.life](https://known.life). It plays two independent roles (see the root
`.life` and `CLAUDE.md`) — here it is purely the *deployment surface*; the rest
of the repo is a .life that happens to use the genepool this worker hosts.

## What it serves

An [Astro](https://astro.build) app with the Cloudflare adapter, deployed as a
single SSR Worker plus edge static assets:

- **The registry API** — the genepool's request-handling core (`publish` ·
  `resolve` · `package` · `claim` · `lifecycle` · `maintainers` · `auth` ·
  `mcp` · `setup` · `github-app` · `skill` · the `/api/index` transparency
  index, mirrored into the public repo so registry history is append-only in
  a git log this worker can't rewrite), which lives in **the
  `known.life/registry` gene** (`imports:` above), materialized into
  `.genome/registry/` and imported by `src/middleware.ts` — the repo holds no
  copy; the gene is the sole source. Its credential-free vitest suite
  (`npm test`) lives in `site/test/`.
- **The docs site** (`src/pages`, `src/layouts`, `src/components`) — prerendered
  to static assets.
- **The multi-life viewer** (served at `/app`) — sign in with GitHub (through
  this worker's own OAuth bridge — no new secrets), open any life you can
  reach LIVE through its own data plane (discovered from its root `.life`
  head's `dataplane:`; the session's identity token is the credential, the
  plane authorizes), and scaffold new lives from the browser. The data plane
  is the only data path — a life without one gets an honest planeless page.
  Lives in **the `known.life/viewer` gene**
  (`imports:` above), materialized into `.genome/viewer/` and mounted as one
  `viewerFetch(request, config)` call from `src/middleware.ts` — the repo
  holds no copy; edit = fetch → mutate → re-vendor, exactly like the registry.
  Its credential-free vitest suite lives in `site/test/viewer-*.test.ts`.
- **The installer** — `install.sh` (repo root) is copied into `public/` at build
  time and served from `known.life/install.sh`, so cold-start never depends on
  raw GitHub. Because that file lives outside this directory, the frontmatter
  declares it under `deploy_inputs:` (engine ≥3.46.0) so an installer edit
  triggers this unit's ci-auto deploy — before that, change detection keyed on
  `site/**` alone and an installer change shipped as a silent no-op.
- **The engine** — shipped as the `life` package's `files["life"]`, served from
  `/api/resolve/life/latest` like any other module (no separate static asset).

`build/` is the build-time half that never ships: `registry-version.mjs` reads
which registry-gene version this deployment vendored out of `.life.lock`, and
`astro.config.mjs` stamps it into the worker through a vite `define` so
`src/middleware.ts` can hand it to the gene on `Env` (the gene serves it as the
MCP handshake's `serverInfo.version`, and cannot know it itself — a Worker has no
filesystem and a gene's version is assigned at publish). It was a literal in the
gene bumped by hand at every publish, which is exactly the kind of value a
structure already holds. Both ends REFUSE to guess: no registry pin in the lock
throws the build, and a deployment that supplies no version fails the handshake
saying so. Neither answers a placeholder — there is no version of "we don't know
what we are" worth serving, so there is no second path to announce (Law 11.3).

## How it deploys

Under the **ci-deploy-convention**: a push that changes `site/**` deploys this
unit from CI via the engine's `deploy --changed`, never by hand from a session
(`life deploy` against the live genepool would break every other .life). The
frontmatter above is the whole deploy spec — the cloudflare adapter absorbs the
build, the Astro module-graph upload, the asset exclusion of `_worker.js`, the
D1 schema apply (`schema.sql`, idempotent), resource-ID resolution by name, and
secret-text carry-through on redeploy. The `config:` overrides bind the live
`known-life-*` resources the worker already reads.

Backing data: **D1** (`known-life-db` — accounts, names, packages, versions,
install_lives, deps, embeddings, `auth_challenge` (lifekey sign-in nonces — one row
per challenge, strongly consistent so concurrent sign-ins don't collide);
`schema.sql`), **KV** (`known-life-known_kv` — caches, rate limits, OAuth state),
**R2** (`known-life-known-r2` —
content-addressed package blobs), **Workers AI** (`env.AI` — bge-base
embeddings powering semantic `/search`, computed lazily and cached in the D1
`embeddings` table; `lib/semantic.ts`). Operator secrets (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,
the OAuth client pair) are set out of band via `data <dir> secret set` and
survive every redeploy.

Observability: the worker emits **Workers Logs + Traces** (declared in the
portable `observe:` knob above; one span per handler/fetch/KV/R2/D1
call). Read the telemetry back from the terminal — no dashboard — with
`node scripts/observe.mjs [--since 6h] [--errors] [--traces] [text]`, which
mints a short-lived token via the cloudflare gene and queries the telemetry API.

The deeper deploy mechanics — the capability→primitive map, `route:` gating, the
Workers REST flow — live in the `cloudflare` skill (`life ask cloudflare`).

<!-- First live deploy through the ci-deploy-convention (deploy.yml → `deploy
--changed` → `life deploy site`), 2026-06-09: an inert change to exercise the
real auto path end-to-end against the live genepool. -->

